    "## Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We predict a strong correlation between higher fast food restaurant density areas and higher adult obesity rates. We also hypothesize that inclusion of other variables like average household income and physical activity rates in these areas would moderate this relationship. \n",
    "\n",
    "This prediction is based on prior datasets and research done on comparing access to food options and health statistics. Previous work has shown that greater exposure to fast food options directly affects the likelihood that residents living in those areas will consume calorie-dense, low-nutrition foods. We also see that fast food restaurants tend to cluster disproportionately in lower income areas. Building on previous knowledge, we would like to confirm that 'food environment' has a genuine correlation with resident health outcomes, specifically obesity. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Our ideal dataset would contain data from various regions across the United States and evaluate the following variables for each location:\n",
    "- Fast food restaurant density\n",
    "- Grocery Store density\n",
    "- Adult obesity (%)\n",
    "- Adult activity rate\n",
    "- Average Household income\n",
    "- Poverty Rate\n",
    "- Food assistance participation\n",
    "- Demographic breakdown (% by race)\n",
    "\n",
    "To cover the variability in all of these variables and to provide an accurate model that we can use to evaluate our hypothesis, we are looking for a dataset of 1500-2000 observations. This data would be collected by synthesizing business records (restaurant count), CDC health surveys (obesity/activity), USDA food assistance data, and census data (income, poverty and demographics). The data would ideally be stored in a normal csv file with the rows as unique locations and the columns describing the above variables for each location. There should be unique ids for each location in case we are working across multiple datasets. \n",
    "\n",
    "Our Possible Datasets:\n",
    "- USDA Food Environment Atlas (Top Choice)\n",
    "\n",
    "https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/food-environment-atlas/data-access-and-documentation-downloads/\n",
    "Free to download as CSV whenever. Provides 3100 entries (1 for each county in the US) with more than 300 variables that cover the ones we are looking for. This single dataset contains all of the desired variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "Variables we want: \n",
    "- fast food restaurants per 1000 residents\n",
    "- Adult obesity rate\n",
    "- Adult physical inactivity rate\n",
    "- Grocery store density\n",
    "- Median household income\n",
    "- Povery rate\n",
    "\n",
    "- CDC PLACES: Local Data for Better Health\n",
    "\n",
    "https://www.cdc.gov/places/index.html\n",
    "Free to download as CSV whenever. Provides 3143 entries (again, county based) that evaluate health outcomes. This is more health-based than the previous dataset. \n",
    "\n",
    "Variables we want:\n",
    "- Adult obesity rate\n",
    "- Adult Physical inactivity rate\n",
    "- Adult diabetes rate\n",
    "\n",
    "- USDA Food Access Research Atlas\n",
    "  \n",
    "https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/food-access-research-atlas/\n",
    "Free to download as CSV whenever. More fine-resolution data; it is based on census data not county summaries. has low-income and low-access population counts and evaluates using the distance from supermarkets and access to vehicles. \n",
    "\n",
    "Variables we want: \n",
    "- low access population counts from 1 mile away\n",
    "- low access population counts from 10 miles away\n",
    "- low income population counts from 1 mile away\n",
    "- low income population counts from 10 miles away\n"
   ]
  },
